                    {
                        "sense_id": "a1981a5c-4498-3eb8-b14c-6d7d7ab8f842",
                        "entry_id": "eff90e66-9927-3f46-957f-2a92d1eb2a67",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Hanging down; drooping, pendulous."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1da82c9f-ecd9-4757-ba01-df40227efe28",
                                "sense_id": "a1981a5c-4498-3eb8-b14c-6d7d7ab8f842",
                                "text": "His flaggy wings when forth he did display, / Were like two sayles, in which the hollow wynd / Is gathered full […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "012b0828-b4ae-3297-865a-09c0ea244435",
                        "entry_id": "eff90e66-9927-3f46-957f-2a92d1eb2a67",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Tasteless; insipid."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "7910b561-f29e-4fc4-9b45-2843c404332c",
                                "sense_id": "012b0828-b4ae-3297-865a-09c0ea244435",
                                "text": "Yet it is reported, that in the Low Countries they will graft an apple cion upon the stock of a colewort, and it will bear a great flaggy apple, the kernel of which, if it be set, will be a colewort, and not an apple."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e9eeb6cf-e8ce-3f5b-a91a-5ef8cf840fc7",
                        "entry_id": "eff90e66-9927-3f46-957f-2a92d1eb2a67",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Tending to split into layers like flagstones."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "08cf79db-95b5-49b2-bce4-bdd5e3496a0b",
                                "sense_id": "e9eeb6cf-e8ce-3f5b-a91a-5ef8cf840fc7",
                                "text": "If this view be correct there must have been a great difference in the sedimentation of the two areas, as the thick beds consist of alternations of flaggy sandstone with occasional true sandstone, almost pure limestones, calmstones, and few or no real flagstones."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "077485e9-a5f6-4aa5-97ea-67d0240861d1",
                                "sense_id": "e9eeb6cf-e8ce-3f5b-a91a-5ef8cf840fc7",
                                "text": "In and out of the tufts they went, with their eyes dilating; wishing to be out of harm, if conscience were but satisfied. And of this tufty flaggy ground, pocked with bogs and boglets, one especial nature is that it will not hold impressions."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6d746ab2-7477-315f-87c5-8b0c11ac7a52",
                        "entry_id": "eff90e66-9927-3f46-957f-2a92d1eb2a67",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Abounding in flags (plants with sword-shaped leaves)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3342f3f0-5198-442b-b1a0-cc3113c2536d",
                                "sense_id": "6d746ab2-7477-315f-87c5-8b0c11ac7a52",
                                "text": "[…] the fish begin to get into drains or ditches, or rushy, flaggy eddies; it is always worth while to run the net round such places at that season, quietly to […] beat the flags out, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3d99326c-0797-41f9-ab3d-61c88f01f29c",
                                "sense_id": "6d746ab2-7477-315f-87c5-8b0c11ac7a52",
                                "text": "There are \"tule\" [reed or rush] rivers, and plains and sloughs and marshes in many parts of North California, the word invariable denoting reedy, rushy, or flaggy."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
